<rml>
<head>
    <title>Recorder</title>
    <link type="text/rcss" href="tokens.rcss"/>
    <link type="text/rcss" href="case.rcss"/>
    <link type="text/rcss" href="mark.rcss"/>
</head>
<body class="overlay">
    <!-- The frame recorder's plate (the user's ask of 2026-09-22): "an overlay element that sits
         it top left just like the fps does in top right". The same plate as the frame rate
         counter, in the opposite corner. Unlike the counter it does NOT move when the title bar
         appears; case.rcss says why, and the short version is that a button which slides out from
         under the pointer aiming at it is not a button.

         THE WHOLE PLATE IS THE BUTTON. One click starts a recording of the length the settings
         row holds, and one more ends it early. The dot is what says which of those a click will
         do, so the state is readable at a glance from across the room rather than needing the
         words to be read. -->
    <div class="counter recorder" id="recorder">
        <div class="mark"></div>
        <span class="counter-value" id="record-text">Record</span>
        <!-- THE DOT IS A DIVIDER AS WELL AS A STATE (the user's ask of 2026-09-23). It sat
             between the mark and the word, where it read as a second mark and left `Record` and
             `0:05` touching each other with nothing between them. Between the two it separates
             them and still carries the color that says whether a click starts or stops. -->
        <span class="dot" id="record-dot"></span>
        <span class="counter-unit" id="record-note"></span>
    </div>
</body>
</rml>
